# Line endings
#
# Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites every text file
# to CRLF on checkout. That is harmless for most files and fatal for two kinds:
#
#   * shell scripts    - a CRLF shebang makes the kernel look for an interpreter
#                        called "/bin/bash\r", which does not exist. Git Bash and
#                        WSL then fail with the famously unhelpful
#                        "bad interpreter: No such file or directory".
#   * batch files      - cmd.exe needs CRLF; an LF-only .bat can mis-parse.
#
# Normalising here means the checkout is correct on every platform regardless of
# how each contributor has configured Git.

# Default: normalise to LF in the repository, let Git decide on checkout.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Must stay LF even on Windows.
*.sh          text eol=lf
*.bash        text eol=lf
*.py          text eol=lf
*.toml        text eol=lf
*.cfg         text eol=lf
*.yml         text eol=lf
*.yaml        text eol=lf
Makefile      text eol=lf

# Must be CRLF: these are executed by Windows shells.
*.bat         text eol=crlf
*.cmd         text eol=crlf
*.ps1         text eol=crlf
*.psm1        text eol=crlf

# Binary — never touch.
*.png         binary
*.jpg         binary
*.jpeg        binary
*.gif         binary
*.pdf         binary
*.ico         binary
*.bin         binary
*.gguf        binary
*.safetensors binary
*.pt          binary
*.pth         binary
*.parquet     binary
*.xlsx        binary
*.xls         binary
*.zip         binary
*.gz          binary

# Keep generated and vendored files out of diffs and language statistics.
paper/**      linguist-documentation
guide/**      linguist-documentation
img/**        linguist-documentation
